---
scope: "Substrate-agnostic subscription filtering, and the hierarchical ALLOCATED namespace identifier that makes it implementable. An entity on ComposeBus DECLARES the semantic messages it wants once, in one substrate-neutral registration; each substrate then ENFORCES that declaration as far UPSTREAM as its physics permits (MQTT at the broker, CAN in the receiver's acceptance filter, a UART child at its bus-side host, in-process nowhere because delivery is already free) and REPORTS which enforcement point it achieved. Covers the enforcement table and its polarity argument, the superset safety invariant, the enforcement-point degradation report, the `[4b root][4b group][8b leaf]` allocated namespace id that replaces `namespace_hash` for the device tier, the prefix-pattern to code+mask lowering, the hardware-measured CAN constraints the lowering must satisfy, the relay pattern (subscription up / messages down / union / one-byte slot compaction), scope through a relay, and the four open decision points."
audience: "The builder implementing the declaration API, the per-substrate enforcement bindings, the namespace-id allocator, or a relay host; the composeux-architect, who owns the four open decision points and must close them before dispatch; David, who ratified the two decisions this spec lowers; anyone who later asks why the device tier stopped hashing namespaces, or why a UART child gets BETTER filtering than a CAN node."
boundary: "IN: the substrate-agnostic declaration and its degradation report ([CXC-SUB-DECLARE], [CXC-SUB-REPORT]); the superset safety invariant ([CXC-SUB-SUPERSET]); the per-substrate enforcement table with its polarity and the rejected MQTT inversion ([CXC-SUB-ENFORCE], [CXC-SUB-NO-INVERT], [CXC-SUB-UNFILTERED]); the allocated hierarchical namespace identifier, its registry, its build-time rules and its lowering to a CAN code+mask ([CXC-NSID-LAYOUT], [CXC-NSID-ALLOCATE], [CXC-NSID-REGISTRY], [CXC-NSID-LOWERING], [CXC-NSID-CAN-WINDOW]); the hardware-measured facts the design rests on ([CXC-SUB-MEASURED]); the relay pattern, slot compaction, scope through a relay, the precision-descends property and the payload-shape boundary ([CXC-SUB-RELAY], [CXC-SUB-COMPACT], [CXC-SUB-SCOPE], [CXC-SUB-PRECISION], [CXC-SUB-PAYLOAD-BOUND]); the error matrix and the two-tier acceptance bar. OUT: the trailing-glob PATTERN LANGUAGE itself and the publisher's reciprocal granularity obligation (reused verbatim from [SPEC:CXC-PATSUB], never re-litigated); the 29-bit identifier layout itself, which is SETTLED — [BP-CAN-ID] v2, ratified 2026-08-13, with [BP-CAN-ID-DIVERGENCE] closed; this spec consumes that layout and records what it costs the CAN filter window ([CXC-NSID-CAN-WINDOW]); foreign-vehicle CAN ingestion ([SPEC:CXC-CAN], a different codec on the same wire); the peer/relay network-segment primitive and its unified emitter contract ([BPR-TRANSPORT-CONTRACT] — consulted, cited, not respec'd); the button_matrix_4x4 board's own firmware (an exemplar here, not a deliverable); MQTT broker administration; CAN-FD, ISO-TP and fragmentation."
not-this: "Not a new subscription model — hierarchical prefix matching is ALREADY ComposeBus's implemented semantics (`python/composeio/bus.py:54`), already declared as `Transport.subscribe_pattern()` ([CXC-FL-PATTERN]) and already surfaced as [CAP-BUS-PATTERN-SUB]. This spec changes the IDENTIFIER and the ENFORCEMENT contract, not the model. Not a replacement for canonical string namespaces — a numeric id is a LOWERING of `bsg/dradis/contact` for substrates that cannot carry a string; MQTT keeps carrying the string and an app that never touches a device tier needs no allocation at all. Not content-based routing — deliberately, and the reason is the whole thesis ([CXC-SUB-WHY-HIERARCHICAL]). Not a design that permits under-delivery anywhere, on any substrate, for any optimisation ([CXC-SUB-SUPERSET]). Not dispatch-ready: FOUR decision points are OPEN and are the architect's to close ([DD-SUB-SLOT-NEGOTIATION], [DD-SUB-RELAY-ROLE], [DD-SUB-BOOT-VS-RUNTIME], [DD-SUB-PUBLISH-SLOTS])."
