# Substrate-Agnostic Subscription Filtering & the Allocated Namespace Identifier [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

> An entity on ComposeBus declares ONCE, in substrate-neutral terms, the semantic messages it wants; each substrate then enforces that declaration as far UPSTREAM as its physics permits and reports which enforcement point it achieved. The declaration is uniform; the mechanism is not, and pretending otherwise would either cripple MQTT or fabricate a filter CAN cannot honour. Two things make this implementable rather than aspirational: the subscription model is hierarchical PREFIX matching, which is the most expressive model that still lowers onto a bitmask; and the namespace identifier becomes ALLOCATED rather than hashed, because a hash is designed to destroy the very correlation between string prefix and bit prefix that a hardware mask needs. One invariant is load-bearing above all others: the enforced filter MUST be a SUPERSET of the declared set, never a subset — over-delivery costs cpu, which is measurable and is the thing being optimised; under-delivery silently drops a message the application asked for and is unrecoverable.

---

## 0. What this spec changes, and what it deliberately does not

### [CXC-SUB-THESIS] The design intent — a leaf must not parse thousands of messages per second to find its own

- premise (David, 2026-08-10, verbatim): "Any entity sitting on the ComposeBus [should] be able to define a
  filter that limits the inbound messages it receives from the bus. To allow it to define the semantic messages
  it wants to receive; this prevents under-powered devices like an ESP32 from having to parse each message it
  receives against a list of messages it can act upon (of which there could be thousands per second). The
  implementation mechanism for this can vary by substrate type (CAN/MQTT/Carrier Pigeon) but the registration
  process for desired messages should be substrate agnostic."
- requirement: a ComposeBus participant MUST be able to express its inbound interest ONCE, in terms that name
  no substrate, and MUST receive an answer stating what was achieved and WHERE. It MUST NOT be required to
  know, at declaration time, whether it is speaking to a broker, a CAN controller, a relay host, or a
  same-process registry.
- rationale: the uniformity belongs in the DECLARATION, not the mechanism — the narrow waist ([DX-NARROW-WAIST])
  applied to subscription. A uniform mechanism would have to be the intersection of every substrate's
  capability, which is "none" (carrier pigeon), and a per-substrate declaration would push substrate knowledge
  into every application that wants to be portable.
- consequence: this spec is the layer that makes [CAP-BUS-PATTERN-SUB]'s CAN path buildable. That capability
  is currently `maturity: partial` with the flag "the CAN hardware-mask path ([CXC-PS-CAN]) is spec'd-not-built";
  the missing piece was never the pattern language, it was the identifier ([CXC-NSID-ALLOCATE]).
- documents: [DEC-SUBSTRATE-AGNOSTIC-SUBSCRIPTION-FILTERING]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

### [CXC-SUB-NOT-NEW] The subscription model is ALREADY ComposeBus's semantics — this spec reuses it by reference

- definition: hierarchical prefix matching is implemented, shipped, and unchanged by this spec. Nothing in the
  semantic model needed to move; David's own conceptualization matched what the code already does.

| Layer | Where it already exists | What it already does |
|-------|------------------------|----------------------|
| Match rule | `python/composeio/bus.py:54` — `return namespace == prefix or namespace.startswith(prefix + "/")` | `p/*` matches `p` and everything under `p/` |
| Pattern grammar | `python/composeio/bus.py:42-64` — `is_pattern`, `pattern_matches`, `validate_pattern` | trailing glob ONLY; a mid-pattern glob raises |
| Transport primitive | `python/composeio/transport.py:186-197` — `Transport.subscribe_pattern(pattern)` | widens what ARRIVES; delivery still flows through `subscribe_all()` dispatch; default no-op |
| MQTT binding | `python/composeio/transport_mqtt.py:167-188` — `_pattern_topic` / `subscribe_pattern` | `jumpdrive/*` -> `{prefix}/+/jumpdrive/#`, subscribed at the broker with `qos=1` |
| Specification | [SPEC:CXC-PATSUB] — [CXC-PS-PRIMITIVE], [CXC-PS-TRANSPORT], [CXC-PS-CAN] | the primitive, the three bindings, the hardware-mask design |
| Capability surface | [CAP-BUS-PATTERN-SUB] | `maturity: partial` — MQTT built, CAN spec'd-not-built |

- requirement: an implementer MUST NOT introduce a second pattern language, a second match function, or a
  parallel subscription registry. Every new mechanism in this spec attaches BELOW `subscribe_pattern()` or
  BESIDE it, never in place of it.
- what is genuinely new here, and it is exactly three things: (1) the enforcement CONTRACT — that enforcement
  is per-substrate, upstream-biased, superset-safe, and reported by enforcement point ([CXC-SUB-ENFORCE],
  [CXC-SUB-SUPERSET], [CXC-SUB-REPORT]); (2) the ALLOCATED namespace identifier that lets a prefix pattern
  become a bitmask at all ([CXC-NSID-ALLOCATE]); (3) the RELAY, which extends the filtering stack across a
  link where only one side sits on the bus ([CXC-SUB-RELAY]).
- see-also: [CXC-PS-PRIMITIVE], [CXC-FL-PATTERN], [CAP-BUS-PATTERN-SUB]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

### [CXC-SUB-WHY-HIERARCHICAL] Hierarchical is not one model among several — it is the CEILING that still lowers onto a bitmask

- claim: hierarchical prefix subscription is the MOST EXPRESSIVE subscription model that can still be pushed
  into silicon. That is the reason to choose it, and it is a stronger reason than familiarity or MQTT parity.
- rationale: a hardware acceptance filter is a comparison of an identifier against a code under a per-bit mask
  ([CXC-SUB-MEASURED] M1, M5). A hierarchical name whose levels occupy fixed bit-fields makes "this subtree" a
  contiguous, maskable set. Content-based routing — subscribe by a predicate over the PAYLOAD — is strictly
  more expressive and cannot be pushed into a CAN acceptance filter at all for extended frames, because the
  extended-frame filter has no data-byte inputs whatsoever (measured: [CXC-SUB-MEASURED] M1). A content-based
  bus therefore obliges every leaf to receive and parse every message to evaluate the predicate — which is
  precisely the thousands-per-second parsing [CXC-SUB-THESIS] exists to prevent.
- principle (worth generalising): **the subscription model determines what can be enforced in silicon.** The
  choice of subscription semantics is not a convenience decision taken at the API layer; it sets a hard ceiling
  on every downstream substrate's ability to help. Choose the model at the ceiling, then lower it.
- boundary: richer matching is not forbidden, it is DEMOTED — a client-side refinement over an
  already-filtered stream ([CXC-PS-CAN]'s broad-mask-plus-software-pass fallback), never a transport
  requirement and never something a leaf must run over unfiltered traffic.
- contrast-with: [CXC-PS-DISCOVERY-VS-SUB]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

---

## 1. The declaration and its answer

### [CXC-SUB-DECLARE] One registration, substrate-neutral, answered with an enforcement report

- definition: a participant declares a DECLARED SET — an ordered list of trailing-glob patterns and exact
  namespaces, per [CXC-PS-PRIMITIVE]'s grammar — plus, for a device-tier participant, the direction each entry
  applies to. The declaration names no substrate, no mask, no topic, no slot.
- requirement: the declaration API MUST be the existing `subscribe_pattern()` waist extended to RETURN an
  enforcement report ([CXC-SUB-REPORT]) rather than `None`. A transport that cannot enforce MUST still return
  a report; returning nothing is indistinguishable from success and is forbidden.
- requirement: the declared set MUST be expressible declaratively in a `.compose.yaml` (the device's config,
  fetched at boot per [CAP-CONFIG-FETCH-BOOT]), not only imperatively in code — a leaf's interests are
  configuration, and a firmware rebuild is not an acceptable cost for adding a subscription.
- default: an empty declared set means SUBSCRIBE NOTHING, not subscribe everything. A participant that wants
  everything declares `*` explicitly. Rationale: the failure mode of the opposite default is the exact
  firehose this spec exists to end, arriving silently at a device that forgot to configure itself.
- default: declaration happens at BOOT, before the transport starts carrying application traffic (see
  [DD-SUB-BOOT-VS-RUNTIME] for the runtime case, which is OPEN).
- error-case: a pattern that fails `validate_pattern()` (a mid-pattern glob) MUST raise at declaration time,
  as it does today (`bus.py:58-64`), not be silently narrowed or widened.
- error-case: a declared set that the substrate cannot enforce even partially MUST produce a report with
  `enforcement: none` and a WARNING log naming the substrate — never a silent pass ([CXC-SUB-UNFILTERED]).
- scenario:
  - when: a device declares `["ship_condition/*", "system/alert"]` and is then attached to an MQTT transport,
    and on another day to a CAN transport, and on a third to a UART link behind a relay host
  - then: the declared set is byte-identical in all three configs; only the returned report differs, and in
    every case the device receives at least `ship_condition`, everything under `ship_condition/`, and
    `system/alert`
- see-also: [CXC-SUB-REPORT], [CXC-SUB-SUPERSET], [CXC-PS-PRIMITIVE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

### [CXC-SUB-REPORT] The degradation report names the ENFORCEMENT POINT, not just coverage

- requirement: the report returned by a declaration MUST state, PER DECLARED ENTRY, which enforcement point
  carried it. A coverage-only report ("4 of 5 filtered") is insufficient and MUST NOT be shipped.
- rationale: enforcement points have materially different cost profiles, and a device budgeting cpu needs to
  know which one it got. "3 at the broker, 1 in silicon, 1 in software" is three different bills: the broker
  entry costs the leaf nothing at all; the silicon entry costs bandwidth but zero cpu (measured — the RX ISR
  never runs, [CXC-SUB-MEASURED] M4); the software entry costs one comparison per arriving frame, which is the
  cost this whole design exists to bound.
- schema (the enforcement-point enum, closed):

| `enforcement` | Meaning | Leaf cost per non-matching message |
|---------------|---------|-----------------------------------|
| `broker` | a source-side fabric declined to send it | zero — it never left the source |
| `host` | a bus-side relay host declined to forward it over the child's link | zero on the child; one host-side comparison |
| `silicon` | a receiver's hardware acceptance filter dropped it | zero cpu; full bandwidth and radio/wire energy |
| `software` | the residue after a broader hardware/fabric filter, discarded in the node | one comparison per message |
| `none` | no filtering exists on this link, and that is DECLARED | one comparison per message, over the whole link's traffic |

- requirement: the report MUST be published to the bus, not only returned in-process — namespace
  `_bus/subscription/report` (infrastructure namespace, per DP-CIO-17), so a monitor or the Composer can see
  what a fleet actually achieved without interrogating each device.
- requirement: a report entry whose `enforcement` is `software` or `none` MUST also carry the estimated
  residue — the set (or pattern) of traffic the leaf will have to discard — so the cpu bill is legible before
  it is paid, not inferred from a profiler afterwards.
- default: `enforcement: software` is the safe assumed value when a transport has not been taught to report;
  it is the pessimistic reading and it never overstates what was achieved.
- scenario:
  - when: a leaf on CAN declares five entries, and its controller's single acceptance filter can express only
    a two-level prefix covering three of them exactly, admitting the other two plus residue
  - then: the report reads three entries `silicon`, two entries `software` with their residue patterns, and
    the leaf's operator can see the cpu bill before the device ships
- see-also: [CXC-SUB-ENFORCE], [CXC-SUB-UNFILTERED]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

---

## 2. The safety invariant — the load-bearing requirement of this spec

### [CXC-SUB-SUPERSET] The ENFORCED filter MUST be a SUPERSET of the DECLARED set, on every substrate, always

- requirement (LOAD-BEARING; every other requirement in this spec yields to it): for every participant, on
  every substrate, at every instant including during reconfiguration, the set of messages the enforcement
  mechanism admits MUST be a superset of the set the participant declared. Under-delivery is FORBIDDEN. There
  is no optimisation, no filter-bank shortage, no bandwidth argument and no boot-time convenience that
  licenses a subset.
- rationale: the two failure directions are not symmetric, and the asymmetry is total. Over-delivery costs
  cpu — bounded, measurable, profilable, and precisely the quantity this design is optimising, so a regression
  shows up as a number. Under-delivery silently drops a message the application ASKED FOR: there is no error,
  no counter, no log line, and no recovery, because the leaf never learns the message existed. A design that
  can silently drop a subscribed message has re-created the class of defect the whole platform keeps paying
  for — a surface that looks healthy while the thing it reports is absent.
- criticality: high
- consequence for masks: when a declared set cannot be expressed exactly, the mask MUST be WIDENED to the
  nearest expressible superset and the residue rejected in software. It MUST NOT be narrowed to the nearest
  expressible subset, even when the excluded entry looks unimportant. [CXC-PS-CAN]'s graceful fallback is
  therefore not merely a nicety — it is the mandated resolution of every expressibility shortfall.
- consequence for transitions: a reconfiguration that passes through an intermediate state MUST pass through
  a WIDER one, never a narrower one. Widen first, then narrow; never narrow first ([DD-SUB-BOOT-VS-RUNTIME]).
- consequence for relays: a relay's upstream subscription MUST be the UNION of its children's declared sets
  plus its own ([CXC-SUB-RELAY]). A union is the smallest superset; anything smaller under-delivers to some
  child.
- testability: this is checkable on every substrate, carrier pigeon included, by the same procedure — publish
  the declared set plus a control set, assert every declared message arrived. It needs no knowledge of the
  mechanism, which is what makes it the one acceptance criterion that ports everywhere.
- scenario:
  - when: a leaf declares `["throttle/left", "system/alert"]` on a CAN controller with ONE single-mode
    acceptance filter, which cannot express two disjoint exact namespaces in one bank
  - then: the loaded mask is the narrowest expressible SUPERSET of both (in the worst case, accept-all), the
    residue is rejected in software, the report reads `silicon` + `software`, and BOTH declared namespaces are
    delivered — never one exactly and the other not at all
- upholds: [CXC-SUB-THESIS]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

---

## 3. Enforcement is per-substrate, as far UPSTREAM as physics permits

### [CXC-SUB-ENFORCE] The enforcement table — polarity follows the fabric, not a convention

- definition: each substrate lowers the same declared set in whichever direction its fabric natively sits.
  Enforcement happens as far UPSTREAM as the physics permits, because everything saved upstream is saved for
  every downstream resource at once — bandwidth, radio energy, wire energy, interrupt load, and cpu.

| Substrate | Where the fabric sits | Enforcement point | Polarity | Precision available | Saves | Mechanism |
|-----------|----------------------|-------------------|----------|--------------------|-------|-----------|
| **MQTT** | a broker between peers | `broker` | **source-side** | arbitrarily precise (native topic wildcard) | bandwidth, radio, power AND cpu | `subscribe_pattern` -> `{prefix}/+/{ns}/#` at `qos=1` (`transport_mqtt.py:179-187`) |
| **CAN** | a shared broadcast wire | `silicon` | **sink-side** | limited by filter geometry: one EXACT 29-bit maskable pattern, OR two patterns on ID[28:13] only — which under [BP-CAN-ID] v2 reaches `root`+`group`+one leaf bit, NOT an exact namespace ([CXC-NSID-CAN-WINDOW]) | cpu ONLY — the interrupt never fires ([CXC-SUB-MEASURED] M4) | acceptance code+mask over the namespace field ([CXC-NSID-CAN-WINDOW]) |
| **UART, bus-side host present** | **the HOST is the fabric** | `host` | **source-side** | **arbitrarily precise** — software matching, no mask geometry, no filter-bank count | the child's wire bandwidth, cpu AND RAM | the host holds the child's declared set and simply does not send ([CXC-SUB-RELAY]) |
| **in-process** | the same address space | `broker` (degenerate) | n/a | exact | nothing to save — delivery is a dict lookup and a call | the bus registry prefix-matches (`bus.py:47-55`); `Transport.subscribe_pattern` correctly no-ops |
| **UART, neither side on the bus** | there is no fabric | `none` | n/a | none | nothing | none, and it MUST be declared ([CXC-SUB-UNFILTERED]) |

- requirement: every Transport implementation MUST declare its enforcement point and its precision limits.
  A transport that inherits `subscribe_pattern`'s default no-op MUST report `software` or `none` honestly; the
  inherited default is correct ONLY for in-process, where delivery is genuinely free.
- key-insight (and it contradicts the initial reading of UART): a UART link with a bus-side host is BETTER
  than CAN, not worse. CAN is a shared broadcast medium — every node receives every bit electrically whether
  it wants to or not, so its filter can only sit in the receiver and can only save the interrupt. A host on the
  bus side of a UART can simply DECLINE TO SEND, which is source-side, and it matches in software, so it has
  no mask geometry and no filter-bank limit at all. "UART has no filtering fabric" is wrong: the host IS the
  fabric.
- corollary: CAN's sink-side polarity is a consequence of physics, not of an API choice, and it is the ONLY
  substrate here whose bandwidth cost is unavoidable. Every claim about CAN's filtering in this spec is a claim
  about saving the CPU, never about saving the wire.
- see-also: [CXC-PS-TRANSPORT], [CXC-SUB-REPORT]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

### [CXC-SUB-NO-INVERT] REJECTED — inverting MQTT to CAN's polarity for uniformity

- rejected-design: make MQTT behave like CAN — subscribe `composeio/#` once and filter client-side — so that
  every substrate shares one sink-side mechanism.
- feasibility: technically trivial. `subscribe_pattern("*")` already produces exactly this topic
  (`transport_mqtt.py:174-175`), so the inversion is a one-line change, which is precisely why it must be
  refused in writing rather than left to a future implementer's taste.
- rationale for rejection: it imports CAN's limitation without CAN's necessity. It would make a leaf's cpu
  cost proportional to TOTAL bus traffic rather than to its own subscription — the exact scaling property
  [CXC-SUB-THESIS] is written to prevent — and it would manufacture the thousands-per-second parsing problem
  on the one substrate currently free of it. Uniformity of MECHANISM is not a goal of this design; uniformity
  of DECLARATION is ([CXC-SUB-THESIS]), and it is already achieved.
- consequence: a transport MUST NOT widen its wire subscription beyond the union of its declared patterns in
  order to simplify its own dispatch. Note that today's bus-level wildcard (`bus._wire_transport_incoming`
  calling `subscribe_all`) does NOT violate this: `MQTTTransport.subscribe_all` only appends to an in-process
  callback list (`transport_mqtt.py:163-165`) and issues no broker subscription, so the socket still receives
  only the declared topics.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

### [CXC-SUB-UNFILTERED] Two peers over UART with neither side on the bus get NO filtering — and it must be DECLARED

- definition: when two ComposeApps are linked over UART and neither sits on the shared bus, there is no fabric
  between them and therefore no enforcement point. Every message crosses the link and each side discards what
  it did not declare.
- requirement: this configuration MUST report `enforcement: none` and MUST be treated as a DECLARED topology,
  not as a degraded relay. A relay ([CXC-SUB-RELAY]) has a bus-side host; this does not, and describing it as
  a broken relay would invite someone to "fix" it by adding a host that the deployment does not have.
- acceptability: acceptable for small-scale deployments (David, 2026-08-10). The cost is bounded by the two
  peers' own traffic, which is the whole link's traffic — an entirely different quantity from a shared bus's
  traffic, and the reason this is tolerable here and not on MQTT.
- error-case: a deployment that declares `enforcement: none` and then grows past its small scale has no
  in-band signal that it did so. A node in this topology SHOULD publish its inbound message rate so the
  crossing point is visible; it MUST NOT silently become the firehose it was exempted from.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

---

## 4. The namespace identifier — hierarchical and ALLOCATED

### [CXC-NSID-LAYOUT] `[4b root][4b group][8b leaf]` in the 16-bit namespace field

- definition: the 16-bit namespace field of a device-tier identifier is divided into three fixed-width levels.
  A canonical namespace string of at most three levels lowers to exactly one 16-bit value.

| Field | Bits (within the 16-bit ns field) | Width | Space | Owner |
|-------|-----------------------------------|-------|-------|-------|
| `root` | 15..12 | 4 | 16 values, of which 0 is RESERVED -> 15 assignable domains | central registry ([CXC-NSID-REGISTRY]) |
| `group` | 11..8 | 4 | 16 per domain | the owning application |
| `leaf` | 7..0 | 8 | 256 per group -> 4096 namespaces per domain | the owning application |

- default (STATED, because an unstated width is a guess a builder will make): **`ROOT_BITS = 4`**. The 5-bit
  root variant David costed (32 domains, 2048 per app) is available but is NOT the default, and taking it
  requires re-cutting the two levels below it — 16 bits minus a 5-bit root leaves ELEVEN, which does not
  accommodate `[4b group][8b leaf]`; it forces either `[3b group][8b leaf]` (8 groups) or `[4b group][7b leaf]`
  (128 leaves). The decision node states the root as "4-5b" without resolving that consequence; this spec
  resolves it by taking 4 and recording the arithmetic, rather than leaving a builder to discover it.
- requirement: two levels of app-owned structure, not three. Fixed-width levels waste bits on shallow subtrees,
  and 12 bits gets cramped at three levels. If it proves tight, WIDEN THE NS FIELD; do not add a level.
- requirement: `root = 0` is RESERVED and MUST NOT be allocated. An all-zero namespace field is therefore never
  a valid namespace, which makes a zeroed or uninitialised identifier detectable rather than plausible.
- default: `group = 0` and `leaf = 0` denote the level ABOVE — `[root][0][0]` is the domain's own top-level
  namespace and `[root][group][0]` is the group's own namespace. This is what makes a prefix subscription a
  pure mask: "everything under root R" is `code = R<<12` with the low 12 bits don't-care, and it admits the
  parent namespace as well, exactly matching `bus.py:54`'s `namespace == prefix or startswith(prefix + "/")`.
- error-case: a canonical namespace with MORE than three levels (`bsg/dradis/contact/bearing`) has NO device-
  tier identifier. It MUST be refused at allocation time with a build-time error naming the namespace. It MUST
  NOT be truncated, hashed, or assigned a synthetic leaf — a silent lowering that loses a level would route
  two distinct namespaces to one id.
- scenario:
  - when: an app declares `bsg/dradis/contact` with `bsg` allocated root 1, `dradis` allocated group 1, and
    `contact` allocated leaf 7
  - then: the device-tier identifier is `[1][1][7]` = `0x1107`, and a subscription to `bsg/dradis/*` is the
    single comparison `(ns & 0xFF00) == 0x1100`
- documents: [DEC-NAMESPACE-ID-ALLOCATED-HIERARCHICAL]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

### [CXC-NSID-ALLOCATE] ALLOCATE, do not hash — this replaces `namespace_hash` for the device tier

- requirement: every segment of a device-tier namespace MUST be assigned an explicit numeric id from a table
  in the owning application's spec/config (`dradis = 1`, `contact = 7`). Segment ids MUST NOT be derived from
  the segment name by any hash, checksum, or digest.
- what this replaces, and only for whom: `composeio.config.namespace_hash` (`python/composeio/config.py:384`)
  is CRC-16/CCITT-FALSE over the WHOLE namespace string. It remains correct for any use that needs a compact
  opaque token; it is REPLACED as the source of the 16-bit device-tier namespace field, which is what
  `transport_can.py` (`_ns_hash`, `encode_arbitration_id`) and `services/can_mqtt_bridge` consume today.
- rationale (the direct fix for the defect that prompted the whole analysis): a hash is DESIGNED to destroy
  the correlation between string prefix and bit prefix. That correlation is exactly what a hardware mask needs.
  Under CRC-16, `throttle/left` and `throttle/right` land at unrelated bit patterns and NO acceptance mask can
  express "the throttle subtree" — not because the mask is weak but because the encoding deliberately removed
  the structure the mask reads. Allocation makes subtree masking possible by construction.
- rationale (collisions): a hash's collisions are silent and are discovered on a bus at 2 a.m. An allocation
  table's collisions are impossible by construction within a group and detectable at build time across one —
  two names cannot receive the same id if the allocator refuses to issue one twice.
- precedent: this is what DBC files and CANopen object dictionaries ARE, and it is why those ecosystems do not
  have this problem. The novelty here is only that ComposeBus keeps the canonical STRING as the primary name
  and treats the number as a lowering ([CXC-NSID-LOWERING]).
- error-case: a duplicate `(root, group, leaf)` in an allocation table MUST be a BUILD-TIME error that fails
  the config compile, never a runtime warning.
- error-case: a namespace arriving on a device-tier substrate with NO allocation MUST be dropped LOUDLY, with
  the namespace named in the log. It MUST NOT fall back to `namespace_hash` — a fallback would silently
  reintroduce the exact defect this node removes, at the one moment nobody is looking.
- error-case: a namespace whose allocation exists but whose root is not in the registry MUST be refused at
  build time ([CXC-NSID-REGISTRY]).
- scenario:
  - when: an app config allocates `throttle` group 3, `left` leaf 1 and `right` leaf 2, under root `bsg` = 1
  - then: the two namespaces are `0x1301` and `0x1302`, a subscription to `bsg/throttle/*` is
    `code = 0x1300` comparing the high 8 bits, and it admits exactly the throttle subtree and nothing else —
    the case that was unexpressible under CRC-16
- documents: [DEC-NAMESPACE-ID-ALLOCATED-HIERARCHICAL]
- see-also: [CXC-NSID-LOWERING], [BP-CAN-ID]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

### [CXC-NSID-REGISTRY] Root ids are centrally assigned per DOMAIN, the way port numbers are

- definition: a small central registry assigns one root id per domain. Structure BELOW the root is owned
  entirely by the owning application and needs no central coordination.

| Root id | Domain | Note |
|---------|--------|------|
| 0 | RESERVED | never allocated; an all-zero ns field is invalid ([CXC-NSID-LAYOUT]) |
| 1 | `bsg` | Battlestar / DRADIS game domain |
| 2 | `zb` | ZombieBox domain |
| 3 | `ic` | Intelligence Console domain |
| 4..15 | unassigned | assigned on request; 12 remaining under `ROOT_BITS = 4` |

- rationale (why root-per-domain is the right cut): generic infrastructure — a bridge, a monitor, the bus core
  — only ever needs to mask on the ROOT ("all bsg traffic"), with zero knowledge of any app's internals.
  Anything needing subtree precision WITHIN an app is by definition a device of that app, which knows its own
  schema. The only thing lost is cross-app subtree subscription, which is a design smell anyway.
- requirement: the registry MUST live in ONE place and be read, not copied. A domain's own spec/config MUST
  reference its assigned root rather than restating it, so a re-assignment is one edit.
- consequence, accepted knowingly (David, 2026-08-10): root in the high bits means DOMAIN determines CAN
  arbitration priority. His words: "extremely unlikely that BSG and ZB traffic would be on actual canbus hw,
  but BSG and some other non-BSG physical devices could be on canbus. But in that case, I'd accept the
  prioritization." Within-app priority remains app-defined, which is correct — an app places something
  priority-shaped high in its own 12 bits.
- error-case: an unregistered root id in a device-tier config MUST fail the build, naming the root and the
  registry location.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

### [CXC-NSID-LOWERING] The numeric id is a LOWERING of the canonical string, never a replacement

- requirement: `bsg/dradis/contact` remains the canonical name of the namespace everywhere — in configs, in
  bindings, in the Composer, in logs, on MQTT. `[1][1][7]` is its encoding for substrates that cannot carry a
  string.
- requirement: MQTT MUST continue to carry the string and MUST NOT be taught the table. An app that never
  touches a device tier needs NO allocation at all — pay for what you use.
- requirement: the lowering MUST be total and reversible within a bus segment: given the allocation table, id
  -> string is a lookup, and a device-tier receiver MUST be able to present the canonical string in a log or a
  report even though it never parsed one off the wire.
- consequence for the relay: a relay host holds the table on behalf of its children, which is what lets a
  2 KB child participate in a string-named bus without ever seeing a string ([CXC-SUB-COMPACT]).
- key-insight: this is the same shape as [CXC-CAN-TWO-CANS]' distinction between a wire encoding and a bus
  meaning — the load-bearing artifact is the namespace, and the identifier is a transport detail. Treating the
  number as primary is what would make the table a second source of truth.
- see-also: [CXC-NSID-ALLOCATE], [DX-NARROW-WAIST]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

### [CXC-NSID-CAN-WINDOW] Under `[BP-CAN-ID]` v2 the namespace field sits at ID[21:6] — the dual-filter window NO LONGER coincides with it

- fact: the ratified identifier layout [BP-CAN-ID] is **v2, `prio(3) | type(4) | ns_id(16) | src(4) | scope(2)`**, placing the namespace field at bits **21..6** of the 29-bit extended identifier. The dual-filter mode of the ESP32-C3 TWAI controller compares EXACTLY ID[28:13] and nothing else — measured ([CXC-SUB-MEASURED] M3), consistent with C3 TRM v1.4 section 31.4.6.2.
- corrected-2026-08-14: ⛔ **THE CLAIM THIS NODE CARRIED UNTIL NOW — "the two windows coincide exactly", and a leaf "gets TWO exact accept patterns at ANY granularity" — IS FALSE UNDER v2.** It was true only under v1 (`[ns 16][type 4][src 9]`), where the namespace field WAS ID[28:13]. v2 ratified 2026-08-13 moved `ns_id` down to ID[21:6] for sound reasons, and nobody redid this arithmetic when it landed; two independently dispatched agents found it the following day. This node is the justification for the entire allocation scheme, so a stale version of it would be BUILT UPON.
- consequence: the windows overlap by nine bits and no longer coincide. ID[28:13] covers `ns_id` bits **15..7** only — `root`(4) + `group`(4) + **the single top bit of `leaf`** — because ns bit 15 lands at ID 21 and the window floor at ID 13 lands at ns bit 7. ns bits 6..0 fall BELOW the comparable window entirely and cannot be pinned at any mask setting. **A dual-mode bank therefore cannot express an exact namespace.** Root-only and root+group remain exactly expressible; an exact leaf does not.
- accepted-loss: ratified as `DEC-ACCEPT-LOSS-OF-DUAL-FILTER-EXACT-MATCH` (2026-08-14). **Keep v2's ordering and correct this spec.** Priority-at-top is worth more than dual-mode exact matching: under v1, arbitration priority was a pseudo-random function of the namespace NAME — `shields` lost arbitration to console bounding boxes, and renaming a signal silently re-ranked it. That is a live-control defect; this is a filtering convenience.
- cost-accepted-and-named: a leaf needing **two** exact namespaces in silicon cannot have it on an ESP32 and MUST filter the second in software, reporting `silicon` + `software` per entry ([CXC-SUB-REPORT]). This is a real capability loss, recorded rather than absorbed.
- single-filter-is-unaffected: **single-filter mode still gives ONE exact 29-bit match**, and field position is irrelevant there because all 29 bits are per-bit maskable ([CXC-SUB-MEASURED] M5, measured). A leaf needing one exact namespace in hardware still gets it under v2. The loss is confined to dual mode.
- general-lesson: a wire-layout change is a change whose blast radius must be MEASURED before a second change compounds it, and that measurement did not happen when v2 landed.
- boundary: (corrected 2026-08-14) the 29-bit layout is **no longer open**. [BP-CAN-ID-DIVERGENCE] is CLOSED as of 2026-08-13 and all three implementations — `python/composeio/transport_can.py`, `cyd/src/bus_can_id.h` and `services/can_mqtt_bridge/bridge.py` — speak v2 identically, pinned across all 29 bits by `env:native_can_id` and `python/tests/test_can_wire_format_v2.py`. `transport_can.py`'s old `[4b type][2b scope][16b ns][7b seq]` packing is RETIRED, not a "deviation to correct": v2 supersedes BOTH v1 packings, and `transport_can.py` is now the single declaration of the shifts that the other two derive from.
- requirement (the lowering table a builder implements — this is the whole point of the allocated id):

| Declared pattern | Compared ns bits | `code` (ns field) | ns `mask` (1 = don't care, legacy TWAI polarity) |
|------------------|------------------|-------------------|--------------------------------------------------|
| `*` | none | `0x0000` | `0xFFFF` |
| `<root>/*` | 15..12 | `root<<12` | `0x0FFF` |
| `<root>/<group>/*` | 15..8 | `root<<12` OR-ed with `group<<8` | `0x00FF` |
| `<root>/<group>/<leaf>` (exact) | 15..0 | the full 16-bit id | `0x0000` |

- requirement: the ns code+mask MUST then be positioned into the full 29-bit filter word per the ratified
  identifier layout — **[BP-CAN-ID] v2, so the 16-bit ns field is shifted left by 6** — with all
  non-namespace bits (**`prio`, `type`, `src`, `scope`**) set DON'T-CARE unless the declaration also
  constrained them. Non-namespace bits MUST default to don't-care — pinning them would narrow the enforced set
  below the declared set, violating [CXC-SUB-SUPERSET].
- requirement-dual-filter-refusal: (v2-specific, and it is a REFUSAL not a degradation) in **dual-filter** mode a declaration that
  requires ns bits below 7 — i.e. any exact leaf — is NOT expressible in silicon at all ([CXC-NSID-CAN-WINDOW]
  accepted-loss). An implementation MUST load the narrowest expressible SUPERSET (`root`+`group`+`leaf[7]`),
  reject the residue in software, and REPORT `silicon` + `software` for that entry. It MUST NOT report
  `silicon` alone, which would claim a hardware guarantee the controller cannot give.
- error-case: more declared patterns than the controller has filter units MUST resolve by loading the
  narrowest expressible SUPERSET covering all of them and rejecting the residue in software
  ([CXC-SUB-SUPERSET]), reporting `silicon` + `software` per entry ([CXC-SUB-REPORT]).
- scenario:
  - when: a leaf declares exactly `bsg/dradis/*` on a controller in single-filter mode, under the [BP-CAN-ID]
    **v2** layout
  - then: one bank pins **ID[21:14]** (`root`+`group`, the ns field's bits 15..8 shifted up by 6) and leaves
    every other identifier bit — `prio`, `type`, the leaf byte, `src`, `scope` — don't-care; every
    `bsg/dradis/...` frame is accepted regardless of priority, type, source or leaf, and every other namespace
    is dropped by the silicon without interrupting the CPU
- scenario (the case v2 CHANGED — stated because it is the one a builder will get wrong):
  - when: the same leaf declares an EXACT namespace `bsg/dradis/contact` on a controller in **dual**-filter
    mode
  - then: the bank can pin only ns bits 15..7, so the accepted set is `bsg/dradis/` plus the top bit of the
    leaf byte — **128 sibling leaves are admitted alongside the declared one**, and the residue MUST be
    rejected in software with the entry reported as `silicon` + `software`. Under v1 this declaration was
    exactly expressible; under v2 it is not
- see-also: [CXC-PS-CAN], [BP-CAN-ID], [BP-CAN-ID-DIVERGENCE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

