## 5. The hardware facts this design rests on — MEASURED, not assumed

### [CXC-SUB-MEASURED] ESP32-C3 TWAI acceptance-filter measurements, 2026-08-10

- provenance: `docs/research/CAN-ACCEPTANCE-FILTER-RESULTS.md` on branch
  `wt-agent-ad1ba9c68bf89edab-1786398375-14832`. One ESP32-C3 rev v0.4, MAC `(a device MAC, not published)`, firmware
  `stamp=c8c58d8b`, 500 kbit/s, Track A self-reception loopback with the jumper on GPIO2 to GPIO10. Every
  experiment passed its validity gate (`state=RUNNING`, `bus_error=0`, `msgs_to_tx=0`, `tx_failed=0`).

| # | Measured fact | Evidence | Reference |
|---|---------------|----------|-----------|
| **M1** | An EXTENDED-frame single filter compares the IDENTIFIER ONLY — never data bytes | E1b: `code=0x0000A500 mask=0xFFFF00FF`; `0x024694a0` with `data[0]=0x5A` ACCEPTED, `0x02468b40` with `data[0]=0xA5` REJECTED; a mirror control at `code=0x00005A00` flipped the accepted set exactly | C3 TRM v1.4 sec 31.4.6.1 |
| **M2** | A STANDARD-frame single filter DOES reach data bytes 1-2, and can mask the identifier out entirely | E1c: `code=0x2460A55A mask=0xFFFF0000`; ids `0x123`, `0x456`, `0x7ff` all accepted on matching data alone; `0x123` rejected when a data byte differed | C3 TRM v1.4 sec 31.4.6.1 |
| **M3** | A DUAL filter compares ONLY ID[28:13]; ID[12:0] and RTR are not comparable at all | E1d: `0x02469fff` (differs from target in all 13 low bits) ACCEPTED; `0x0246a000` (differs only in ID[13]) REJECTED; both bank orderings byte-identical, so bank 2 is live | C3 TRM v1.4 sec 31.4.6.2 |
| **M4** | Rejection happens in HARDWARE — the CPU is never interrupted for a rejected frame | E1e: identical 20-frame bursts; accept-all gave `msgs_to_rx=4, rx_missed=16` (queue depth 4), the rejecting filter gave `msgs_to_rx=0, rx_missed=0` with `tx ok=20, tx_failed=0` proving the frames reached the wire | — |
| **M5** | In SINGLE-filter mode, field POSITION is irrelevant — all 29 bits are per-bit maskable | E4: a 2-bit field at ID[24:23] matched exactly across identifiers differing in 27 other bits (`0x00800000` and `0x1effffff` accepted; three neighbours rejected) | — |
| **M6** | Changing the acceptance filter on an installed driver requires a driver RE-INIT | every `FILTER` command issued against an installed driver answered `reinit=1 counters=zeroed`; the legacy ESP-IDF TWAI API takes `twai_filter_config_t` as an argument to `twai_driver_install()`, so there is no in-place path | measured-adjacent (see limits) |

- limits on this evidence, stated so nothing here is over-read:
  - **M6 is measured-adjacent, not a measured bus-off.** The bench proves the driver re-installs on a filter
    change; it does NOT measure how long a live segment loses the node, and no two-node bus existed to measure
    it on. [DD-SUB-BOOT-VS-RUNTIME] treats the re-init as real and its DURATION as unquantified.
  - **All measurements are self-reception on ONE board.** Peer reception across a real two-node bus was not
    run (only one C3 was attached, no transceivers). E1a rules out the gross concern — self-reception plainly
    passes through the filter, since it discriminated single identifier bits — but "identical in every respect
    to peer reception" is not proven.
  - **E2 (arbitration under contention) was not run.** Nothing in this spec infers anything about arbitration
    behaviour from the bench.
  - **The silicon agreed with the TRM on every point tested.** The bench found no documentation defect.
- consequence: M1 is the fact that forecloses content-based routing on CAN ([CXC-SUB-WHY-HIERARCHICAL]); **M3
  is the fact that BOUNDS what dual-filter mode can express under [BP-CAN-ID] v2 — it reaches ns bits 15..7
  only, so an exact leaf is not expressible in dual mode ([CXC-NSID-CAN-WINDOW]). Under v1 the same
  measurement made the namespace placement a windfall; the measurement did not change, the LAYOUT did, and the
  conclusion inverted with it**; M4 is the fact that makes `enforcement: silicon` genuinely free of cpu
  ([CXC-SUB-REPORT]); **M5 is the fact that keeps single-filter mode exact under v2 at any field position,
  which is what makes the dual-mode loss survivable**; M6 is the constraint that shapes
  [DD-SUB-BOOT-VS-RUNTIME].
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

---

## 6. The relay — filtering on behalf of a child that cannot own it

### [CXC-SUB-RELAY] Subscription propagates UP, messages flow DOWN; the host's upstream set is the UNION

- definition: where only one side of a link sits on the bus, the BUS-SIDE HOST implements the filtering stack
  on the child's behalf. The child declares its set over the link at setup; the host adds those entries to its
  own upstream subscription and forwards downward only what the child declared.
- exemplar (real hardware, (a local path, not published)):
  `Host ESP32 --UART--> CH32V003 --I2C--> 2x MCP23017` — U1 at 0x20 reads 16 buttons (INTA to CH32V003 PD0),
  U2 at 0x21 drives 16 LEDs. `J_HOST` is a 4-pin header carrying only VCC / GND / TX / RX (the Grove connector
  `J_GROVE` carries the same four nets at 2.0 mm pitch), so **the UART is the entire channel** — there is no
  side-band, no shared memory, and no second bus to fall back on. The CH32V003 has 2 KB of SRAM.
- requirement: the host's own upstream subscription MUST be the UNION of every child's declared set plus the
  host's own. A union is the smallest superset, and anything smaller under-delivers to some child
  ([CXC-SUB-SUPERSET]).
- requirement: one inbound message MAY fan out to N children. Each child receives it under ITS OWN per-child
  slot id ([CXC-SUB-COMPACT]); slot spaces are per-link and MUST NOT be shared across children.
- requirement: the host MUST NOT forward to a child anything outside that child's declared set, even when the
  host itself subscribed to it. The union is an upstream quantity only; downstream, each link is filtered to
  its own child's declaration.
- error-case: a child declaring a namespace the host cannot subscribe to upstream (unallocated, unregistered
  root, or refused by the upstream substrate) MUST receive an explicit per-entry failure in its setup answer,
  and the host MUST surface it in its own report ([CXC-SUB-REPORT]). A silently-unhonoured child declaration
  is under-delivery with extra steps.
- scenario:
  - when: two children on one host declare `["ui/led/*"]` and `["system/alert"]` respectively, and the host
    itself declares `["net/status"]`
  - then: the host's upstream subscription is exactly `{ui/led/*, system/alert, net/status}`; a `ui/led/3`
    message reaches child 1 only; a `system/alert` reaches child 2 only; `net/status` reaches neither child
- see-also: [CXC-SUB-COMPACT], [CXC-SUB-SCOPE], [DD-SUB-RELAY-ROLE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

### [CXC-SUB-COMPACT] Filtering alone is insufficient for a 2 KB child — the host also COMPACTS to `[slot][value]`

- requirement: at link setup the host MUST assign each of the child's declared namespaces a ONE-BYTE ORDINAL
  (a slot), and the wire MUST then carry `[slot][value]`. The child never parses a namespace string, never
  holds a string table, and never allocates for one.
- rationale: for a 2 KB-SRAM CH32V003, filtering fixes the CPU bill but not the MEMORY bill. A namespace
  string is tens of bytes; a slot is one. The declaration is what makes the compaction possible — the host
  knows the child's complete set of interests precisely because the child declared it — so **both fall out of
  ONE registration**. This is the strongest argument for making declaration mandatory rather than optional:
  it is not only a filter, it is a code book.
- default: slot 0 is RESERVED and means "unmapped / error"; a child receiving slot 0 MUST log and discard,
  never guess. Slot 255 is RESERVED pending [DD-SUB-SLOT-NEGOTIATION]'s answer on the >255 case.
- requirement: the mapping is LINK-LOCAL. A slot number has no meaning off the link and MUST NOT be forwarded
  upstream ([DD-SUB-PUBLISH-SLOTS] covers the publish direction, which is OPEN).
- error-case: a slot arriving for which the child holds no mapping MUST be discarded with a counter increment,
  and the child SHOULD request re-negotiation. It MUST NOT be interpreted positionally against the child's
  own table order.
- scenario:
  - when: a button-matrix child declares 16 LED namespaces `ui/led/0` through `ui/led/15` and the host assigns
    slots 1 through 16
  - then: an inbound `ui/led/7 = 1` crosses the UART as two bytes `[8][1]`, and the child indexes its LED
    array directly with no string parsing, no allocation and no table lookup beyond an offset
- see-also: [CXC-SUB-RELAY], [DD-SUB-SLOT-NEGOTIATION]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

### [CXC-SUB-SCOPE] A child is INSIDE the host's locality — host + children are ONE bus participant with internal structure

- ratified: David, 2026-08-10, resolving the `scope-through-a-relay` question left open in
  [DEC-SUBSTRATE-AGNOSTIC-SUBSCRIPTION-FILTERING].
- definition: locality is the PARTICIPANT, not the process. A host and its UART children form one bus
  participant whose internal structure happens to span a wire.
- requirement (the symmetry, and getting it wrong in either direction is a defect):

| Direction | Rule | Failure if inverted |
|-----------|------|--------------------|
| Host publishes `Scope.LOCAL` | **DOES** cross to children over the UART hop | children miss state the host considers local to itself, so a host-local value cannot drive a child LED |
| Child publishes `Scope.LOCAL` | reaches the host and its SIBLINGS, and **MUST NOT** be relayed upstream | a naive forward-everything relay leaks local chatter onto the shared bus |
| Child publishes `Scope.GLOBAL` | relayed upstream by the host's own transport | — |

- requirement: the relay MUST respect scope on the PUBLISH path exactly as it does on the subscribe path.
  This is `scope`-is-already-the-uplink-control applied one layer down — cf. `bus._send_message`, which
  forwards to the global transport only when scope is global or auto.
- consequence: **the relay is NOT a scope boundary — the host's UPSTREAM transport is.** A builder who places
  the boundary at the relay will suppress legitimate host-to-child local delivery and simultaneously leak
  child-local traffic, because both errors follow from the same misplacement.
- unification (no new concept is introduced by any of this): the internal bus ALREADY spans a process boundary
  — the Intelligence Console runs its whole data layer in-process and `Scope.LOCAL` never leaves. A UART child
  is that same internal bus extended across a physical wire. Same scope semantics, wider extent.
- practical payoff at the leaf: scope becomes an optimisation lever the child can declare. `button_matrix` can
  publish press events as `local` when only the host consumes them, costing the shared bus nothing, while
  `system/alert` arrives as `global` from upstream — the intent-up / state-down split with a bandwidth
  dividend.
- open-point (recorded, NOT blocking): `remote`. [BP-CAN-DATA] byte 0 defines four scope values
  (local / global / remote / auto) and `remote` presumably means cross-segment. This spec does not say whether
  a relay may ORIGINATE or FORWARD a `remote`-scoped message; a builder MUST treat `remote` across a relay as
  unspecified and refuse it loudly rather than choose a behaviour.
- see-also: [CAP-BUS-NAMESPACE-SCOPE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

### [CXC-SUB-PRECISION] Precision INCREASES as you descend the tree — cheapest-node, read in the other direction

- principle: each relay can afford more selectivity than the medium above it, so filtering gets MORE precise
  the further from the shared bus you go. A host on CAN absorbs a coarse hardware mask — limited by filter
  geometry ([CXC-SUB-MEASURED] M3, M5) — and delivers an EXACT set downward to each child, because its own
  matching is software over a string it already holds.
- key-insight: this is the cheapest-node principle inverted. Filtering-for-a-child is a behaviour the child
  CANNOT own — it has neither the fabric nor the memory — so it flows UP to the cheapest node that CAN own it.
  The usual reading pushes behaviour down to the leaf; this is the case where the same reasoning pushes it up,
  and the two are not in tension because the criterion is identical: place the behaviour at the cheapest node
  capable of performing it.
- consequence for design reviews: a relay host that merely forwards its own filtered stream downward has
  wasted the precision it was uniquely able to add, and its children pay for it in cpu and RAM. Forwarding is
  not a simpler correct implementation; it is a worse one.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

### [CXC-SUB-PAYLOAD-BOUND] The substrate-agnostic thesis is bounded by PAYLOAD SHAPE, not by semantics

- claim: substrate-agnosticism holds for SEMANTICS. It does not, and cannot, hold for payload shape.
- exemplar: the Intelligence Console could not ride CAN regardless of filtering. Its payloads
  (`self/deliverables`, the bootstrap manifest) exceed 8 bytes on the first message, which is why
  `CANTransport.send` refuses oversized payloads LOUDLY (`transport_can.py`: a classic CAN frame is 8 bytes,
  fragmentation is not implemented, so it warns rather than shipping half a payload).
- requirement: a spec or config that binds a fat-payload app to a thin substrate MUST fail at configuration
  time with the byte counts named, not at runtime on the first oversized message.
- consequence, and it is the constructive half: **the relay is also how a fat-payload app reaches a thin
  substrate at all** — project the namespace DOWN to a slot carrying a scalar. The console cannot send its
  deliverables list to a CH32V003, but it can send a derived `ui/led/3 = 1`. The projection is an act of
  design, performed at the host, and it is what a relay is for beyond mere filtering.
- see-also: [CXC-SUB-RELAY], [CXC-PS-GRANULARITY]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

---

## 7. Capability facet

### [CAP-BUS-SUBSTRATE-FILTER] Substrate-agnostic subscription filtering — declare once, enforced per substrate at the furthest-upstream point, reported by enforcement point

- kind: capability
- maturity: planned
- flag: spec'd-not-built. This spec is what makes [CAP-BUS-PATTERN-SUB]'s CAN hardware-mask path buildable —
  that capability's blocker was never the pattern language but the hashed identifier ([CXC-NSID-ALLOCATE]).
  FOUR decision points remain OPEN ([DD-SUB-SLOT-NEGOTIATION], [DD-SUB-RELAY-ROLE], [DD-SUB-BOOT-VS-RUNTIME],
  [DD-SUB-PUBLISH-SLOTS]); this capability cannot advance past `planned` until they are closed. Ratify.
- tier-coverage: bus (MQTT source-side, in-process degenerate); device (CAN sink-side in silicon); relay
  (UART child behind a bus-side host); declared-unfiltered (peer UART, neither side on the bus)
- verified-by: none yet — the verification handles are the SUB-C rows of [CXC-SUB-ACCEPT], none of which
  exists. Reads as "spec'd, unverified" by design.
- depends-on: [CXC-SUB-DECLARE]
- depends-on: [CXC-NSID-ALLOCATE]
- see-also: [CAP-BUS-PATTERN-SUB]
- see-also: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]
- part-of: [CAP-MAP]

---

## 8. Decision points — OPEN, the architect's to close before dispatch

### [DD-SUB-SLOT-NEGOTIATION] Slot negotiation over UART — initiator, the >255 case, host restart, and the not-yet-existing namespace

- decision: PROPOSED, not ratified — **the CHILD declares, the HOST allocates.** Four sub-answers, each of
  which a builder would otherwise have to invent: (a) INITIATOR — the child sends its declared set after its
  boot banner; the host replies with the slot map. (b) PAST 255 — REFUSE the 256th declaration loudly and
  report it ([CXC-SUB-REPORT]); slot 0 is `unmapped/error` and slot 255 stays reserved for a future two-byte
  escape. (c) HOST RESTART — the CHILD RE-DECLARES; the host persists nothing and signals a LINK EPOCH that
  increments on host start, and a child seeing a new epoch re-declares. (d) NAMESPACE NOT YET EXISTING — the
  host MUST accept the declaration and assign a slot anyway, answering `assigned, not-yet-seen`.
- rationale: (a) the host cannot know a child's semantic needs, so a host-initiated poll needs the same round
  trip plus a discovery step for no gain. (b) 255 declarations on a 2 KB part is already past the plausible
  budget, and an always-available two-byte escape would double the wire cost of every message against a case
  that should not occur; refusing loudly makes the boundary visible at bring-up instead of at 2 a.m. (c) the
  child OWNS the truth about its interests, and a host-persisted map goes stale silently against a re-flashed
  child — the failure is the wrong LED lighting, with no error anywhere, which is precisely the silent-wrong
  class this platform keeps paying for. (d) liveness is already the pattern primitive's contract
  ([CXC-PS-PRIMITIVE]: family members that appear AFTER the subscription still deliver), so a
  never-yet-published namespace is not an error; answering `not-yet-seen` lets the child distinguish "no data
  yet" from "never will".
- alternatives: (i) HOST-INITIATED POLL — the host asks each child for its set; symmetrical with a discovery
  protocol the platform does not have, and it makes the host responsible for knowing a child exists before the
  child speaks. (ii) STATIC COMPILED SLOT MAP, no negotiation at all — zero runtime cost and zero protocol,
  genuinely attractive for a fixed product, but changing one namespace re-flashes BOTH sides and the map is
  then duplicated in two firmwares (the fork-divergence class). (iii) HOST PERSISTS THE MAP across restart —
  saves one round trip on every boot; costs the stale-map failure in (c). (iv) TWO-BYTE SLOTS ALWAYS — removes
  the >255 boundary entirely at the cost of doubling every message's overhead on the most constrained link in
  the system.
- status: proposed
- invalidating_assumption: "That a child's declared set is SMALL (tens) and STATIC across a power cycle. If
  children begin declaring dynamically at runtime, or if a child legitimately needs more than 255 namespaces,
  the refuse-loudly answer in (b) becomes wrong and the escape slot must be specified; and if re-declaration
  latency ever lands on a human-visible path (a host restart that visibly blanks a panel), (iii) becomes
  right and the staleness must be solved with a map checksum instead."
- documents: [CXC-SUB-COMPACT]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

### [DD-SUB-RELAY-ROLE] Is a relay a first-class ROLE, or a Transport? — and `MAPPED_DEVICE_BUS` already covers more than it looks

- checked-first, as instructed: [BPR-TRANSPORT-CONTRACT] (`docs/composeplatform/bus-peer-relay.lts`;
  `python/composeio/transport_contract.py`) already defines ONE Transport waist with three knobs — QoS,
  directionality, and far-side type — and its `FarSide.MAPPED_DEVICE_BUS` value explicitly covers a far side
  that lacks native bus semantics, where "the bridge maps namespaces to arbitration IDs and
  synthesizes-or-degrades the missing semantics." **RE-ENCODING is therefore already inside the Transport
  contract**, and the premise that "a Transport moves ComposeBus messages across a substrate unchanged" is
  narrower than the contract that actually exists. Slot compaction is a namespace-to-wire-id mapping of the
  same species as namespace-to-arbitration-id.
- what remains genuinely OUTSIDE the existing contract, and it is three things: (1) **RE-SCOPING** — a relay
  applies scope policy on the publish path ([CXC-SUB-SCOPE]), whereas today the BUS decides which transport a
  scope reaches (`bus._send_message` forwards to the global transport only for global/auto) and no Transport
  applies scope itself; (2) **FAN-OUT** — a Transport has ONE far side, a relay has N children with N
  independent slot spaces and N distinct declared sets; (3) **A DERIVED SUBSCRIPTION** — the relay's own
  upstream subscription is a computed union of its children's, so it is not something its owner declares.
- decision: PROPOSED, not ratified — a relay is a **Transport** profiled
  `FIRE_AND_FORGET / BIDIRECTIONAL / MAPPED_DEVICE_BUS`, COMPOSED WITH one new collaborator (a subscription
  aggregator) that owns the union, the per-child declared sets, the per-child slot tables and the scope policy.
  Reuse the contract; add one collaborator; do not fork the waist and do not bury fan-out inside a class whose
  contract says "one far side".
- rationale: this is the only option that satisfies both standing constraints — [BPR-TRANSPORT-CONTRACT]
  exists precisely to prevent a second forked emitter, and the three genuinely-new behaviours above are real
  and must live somewhere legible. A collaborator is the smallest thing that is honest about both.
- alternatives: (a) PURE TRANSPORT, everything inside — cheapest to write; buries fan-out and scope policy in
  a class whose declared contract does not mention either, so the next reader trusts the contract and is
  wrong. (b) FIRST-CLASS RELAY ROLE outside the Transport hierarchy — honest about re-scoping, but forks the
  emitter waist that [BPR-TRANSPORT-CONTRACT] was written to unify, and re-opens the question the transport
  contract already settled. (c) N TRANSPORTS, ONE PER CHILD — makes fan-out free and slot spaces naturally
  per-instance, but the union must live above them anyway (so the collaborator appears regardless) and the
  host then opens N transport objects onto ONE physical UART.
- status: proposed
- invalidating_assumption: "That scope policy on the publish path is the ONLY bus semantic a relay must apply
  which a Transport does not. If a relay must also dedupe, re-sequence, arbitrate between children, or
  originate `remote`-scoped traffic, it has grown a routing table and it IS a bus, not a transport — at which
  point alternative (b) becomes right and the child side should be modelled as a real second ComposeBus
  instance rather than a far side."
- documents: [CXC-SUB-RELAY]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

### [DD-SUB-BOOT-VS-RUNTIME] Boot-time vs runtime subscription, given that a TWAI filter change costs a driver re-init

- measured constraint: changing the acceptance filter on an installed driver requires a driver RE-INIT
  ([CXC-SUB-MEASURED] M6) — a brief bus-off for that node on a live segment. The DURATION was not measured and
  no two-node bus existed to measure it on. Consequence that makes this non-local: a CHILD subscribing at
  RUNTIME widens its host's union ([CXC-SUB-RELAY]), which reconfigures the HOST's controller — so a child's
  cheap-looking runtime subscribe can take its host off a shared CAN segment.
- decision: PROPOSED, not ratified — **BOOT-TIME declaration is the contract; runtime subscription is an
  explicitly-priced exception**, and on CAN specifically the priced exception should be satisfied in SOFTWARE
  rather than by re-loading the mask. Concretely: (i) a runtime widen resolves by refining in software beneath
  an UNCHANGED hardware mask whenever the boot mask is already a superset — which it will be whenever the boot
  mask was widened for expressibility, and it costs no re-init and cannot violate [CXC-SUB-SUPERSET]; (ii) a
  runtime widen that genuinely exceeds the boot mask coalesces over a debounce window (default 250 ms) so N
  children joining together cost ONE re-init; (iii) a node may declare `filter_stability: locked`, after which
  a widen that needs hardware is REFUSED with a report entry rather than silently taking the bus down; (iv)
  NARROWING never requires urgency and MUST be deferred to the next boot, because narrowing is the only
  direction that could pass through a subset state.
- rationale: the asymmetry in [CXC-SUB-SUPERSET] resolves this almost entirely. Widening in software is always
  safe (hardware stays a superset); narrowing in hardware is the only operation that needs a re-init and it is
  never urgent, because an over-wide filter is merely expensive. The lean toward boot-time is therefore not
  conservatism — it is that the runtime case decomposes into one free operation and one deferrable one.
- alternatives: (a) RUNTIME EVERYWHERE, absorb the re-init — simple and uniform; a child hot-joining takes its
  host's controller down mid-flight on a shared segment, which is a fault injected by a feature. (b)
  BOOT-ONLY, no runtime at all — cheapest and safest; a relay host then cannot admit a child that plugs in
  later, which is exactly the button-matrix use case, so it forecloses the deployment shape the relay was
  designed for. (c) SOFTWARE-ONLY WIDENING, hardware mask frozen at boot forever — never a re-init, invariant
  trivially held; the cpu cost of the residue grows unbounded as the widened set drifts from the boot
  prediction, with no mechanism to reclaim it.
- status: proposed
- invalidating_assumption: "That a device's set of interests is knowable at BOOT. If devices become genuinely
  dynamic — a hot-plug child mesh, or a Composer authoring session retargeting a live device's subscriptions
  from the glass — then boot-time-as-contract inverts, the re-init cost must be engineered around rather than
  avoided, and the bus-off duration becomes a number that must be MEASURED on a two-node bus before any of
  this is safe. Also invalidated if a controller with in-place filter update (a non-legacy TWAI driver or
  different silicon) becomes the target, since M6 is a property of this driver and this part."
- documents: [CXC-SUB-DECLARE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

### [DD-SUB-PUBLISH-SLOTS] The publish direction's slot space — separate from subscribe, and does the host translate or forward?

- decision: PROPOSED, not ratified — **SEPARATE slot space per direction**, allocated in the same setup
  exchange (the child declares what it PUBLISHES, with a SCOPE per entry; the host assigns up-slots), and the
  host **TRANSLATES** (slot to canonical namespace string) rather than forwarding the slot upstream.
- rationale (separate spaces): the two directions have different cardinalities and different owners — a
  button matrix subscribes 16 LED namespaces and publishes 16 button namespaces, but a display child may
  subscribe 40 and publish 0. Sharing one 8-bit space halves both for no gain, and it makes an off-by-one in
  one direction corrupt the other. It also removes an allocation conflict: the subscribe direction is
  allocated by the HOST, so a shared space would need two allocators to agree.
- rationale (translate, not forward): a slot is a LINK-LOCAL name. Forwarding it upstream would put a name
  with no meaning off-link onto the shared bus and require every upstream consumer to hold the host's table —
  which is [CXC-NSID-ALLOCATE]'s defect in a new costume, a bit pattern whose meaning is not recoverable from
  the bus. The host holds the canonical string and publishes it, with `src` identifying the child.
- consequence that a builder must not miss: because the host applies scope on the publish path
  ([CXC-SUB-SCOPE]), the child's publish declaration MUST carry a SCOPE PER SLOT — otherwise the host cannot
  tell a child-local press event from one that belongs upstream, and the safe default (treat everything as
  local) would silently strand global events at the host.
- alternatives: (a) ONE SHARED SLOT SPACE, host translates — one table instead of two; halves the space and
  couples the directions. (b) SEPARATE SPACES, host FORWARDS the slot upstream with a child id — cheapest on
  the host; leaks a link-local name onto the shared bus. (c) NO PUBLISH SLOTS AT ALL — the child sends full
  namespace strings upstream. A 2 KB child can hold its own short const string table, and the up direction is
  typically low-rate and change-driven ([CXC-PS-RATE], [CXC-PS-GRANULARITY]), so this costs wire bytes and
  buys zero negotiation in that direction. A genuine contender for children that publish few, rare events.
- status: proposed
- invalidating_assumption: "That the UP direction is low-rate and low-cardinality relative to the DOWN
  direction. If a child becomes a high-rate sensor publishing many namespaces, alternative (c) collapses and
  the publish slot space becomes as load-bearing as the subscribe one — and if BOTH directions then approach
  255, the shared-space alternative (a) becomes actively harmful rather than merely wasteful."
- documents: [CXC-SUB-COMPACT]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-SUBFILTER]

